course: biology
lesson: 258
title: The microscopic world: viruses and vaccines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A virus is far smaller than a bacterium, a tiny packet of instructions in a coat, often drawn like a ball with spikes; most are too small to see even with an ordinary school microscope.
explain: On its own a virus cannot grow, eat or copy itself, so scientists still argue whether it is alive; it has to get inside a living cell and trick the cell into building copies of the virus.
explain: When your friend sneezes and you catch his cold, viruses in tiny drops got into the cells of your nose, and soon those cells were busy making more viruses instead of doing their own job.
explain: A vaccine is like a «wanted» poster for your immune system: it shows the body a harmless piece or a weakened form of a germ, so that when the real germ comes, the body recognises it and fights fast.
explain: In 1796 Edward Jenner made the first vaccine from cowpox, and with vaccines people later wiped smallpox off the whole Earth, which was declared done in 1980.
fact: Colds are caused by viruses spread by sneezes, coughs and touching hands and then faces, and there are over 200 kinds of cold virus, so you keep catching new ones.
fact: A virus gets into a cell and makes the cell build copies of the virus; it cannot copy itself on its own, so scientists argue whether viruses are alive.
fact: Most viruses are much smaller than bacteria and too small to be seen with an ordinary light microscope.
fact: A vaccine shows the immune system a harmless piece or weak form of a germ, so the body learns to recognise it and fights it fast next time; when almost everyone in a place is vaccinated, the germ finds very few people to jump to and struggles to spread.
fact: Edward Jenner made the first vaccine in 1796 from cowpox, and smallpox was declared wiped out worldwide in 1980.
fact: A fever is the body turning up the brain's thermostat to make it harder for germs to multiply; it is usually helpful, but a very high fever needs a doctor.
fact: Masks catch drops from coughs and sneezes that carry viruses, and coughing or sneezing into your elbow keeps germs off your hands.
fact: Bacteriophages are viruses that attack bacteria; they look like tiny moon landers, inject their instructions and burst the bacterium, and doctors sometimes use them when antibiotics fail.
fact: The chickenpox virus can hide in nerves for decades and come back as shingles, often when the immune system weakens with age; there are vaccines for both.
fact: Bats live in big crowded colonies and carry many viruses without getting sick, thanks to very strong immune systems; viruses can sometimes jump from animals to people, but bats are very important for pollination and eating insects and should not be harmed.
fact: The famous stripes on «broken» tulips in old Dutch paintings were caused by a virus, and in the 1630s a single striped bulb could cost as much as a house.
fact: A computer virus is named after real viruses because it copies itself by getting into a host, a program instead of a cell.
fact: About 8 percent of human DNA is made of bits of old viruses that got into our ancestors long ago.
fact: Giant viruses such as the mimivirus, described in 2003, are bigger than some small bacteria, and some are even infected by their own little viruses.
exercise: How do cold viruses usually spread? | By sneezes, coughs and touching hands and faces | By eating cold food and drinking icy drinks in winter | By going outside with wet hair on a cold, windy winter day | By sitting in a cold room without a warm jumper on
exercise: Why do you keep catching new colds? | There are over 200 kinds of cold virus | Your body forgets how to fight every germ | Colds come from the weather changing | Every cold is the same virus coming back
exercise: What does a virus do inside a cell? | Makes the cell build copies of the virus | Eats the cell from the inside, bit by bit | Grows into a bigger bacterium | Turns the cell into a muscle
exercise: Why do scientists argue whether viruses are alive? | They cannot grow or copy themselves without a living cell | They are made of tiny bits of metal and glass, not of living stuff | They live for only one second | They are too big to be alive, so they count as rocks
exercise: How does a virus compare in size with a bacterium? | It is usually much smaller | It is usually much bigger than a bacterium | They are always the same size | It is the size of a grain of sand
exercise: How does a vaccine help the body? | It teaches the immune system to recognise a germ | It kills every germ in the body at once, good and bad | It makes a wall around each cell | It gives you the illness so you get it over
exercise: Who made the first vaccine, in 1796? | Edward Jenner | Alexander Fleming | Louis Pasteur | Robert Hooke
exercise: Which disease was wiped out worldwide by 1980? | Smallpox | The common cold | Flu | Chickenpox
exercise: Why does the body make a fever? | To make it harder for germs to multiply | Because the heart is working far too slowly | To cook the food sitting in the stomach faster | Because the blood has turned much too thick
exercise: Why is it better to sneeze into your elbow than your hand? | It keeps germs off the hands that touch things | Elbows have special skin that kills viruses on contact | The sneeze is quieter that way | Hands are too small to catch a sneeze
exercise: What are bacteriophages? | Viruses that attack bacteria | Bacteria that hunt and eat viruses | White blood cells that live in the gut | Medicines made from bread mould
exercise: What causes shingles in older people? | The chickenpox virus waking up after hiding in nerves | A new virus caught from the bats that live up in the attic | Too much sun on the skin | Eating too many sweets and sugary drinks every day
exercise: Why can bats carry many viruses without falling ill? | They have very strong immune systems | They never meet any viruses | Viruses cannot fly | Bats are not really animals
exercise: What caused the beautiful stripes on Dutch «broken» tulips? | A virus | A painter's brush | Cold winters in Holland | Mixing two kinds of soil
exercise: Why is a computer virus called a virus? | It copies itself by getting into a host, like a real virus | It is made from real viruses | It can make people very sick if they open it on a computer | It was found in a hospital
exercise: About how much of your DNA comes from old viruses? | About 8 percent | None at all | About half | All of it
exercise: What surprised scientists about the mimivirus? | It is bigger than some small bacteria | It is the smallest thing ever found | It glows in the dark | It lives only on the Moon
test: «my mate says a virus is just a really tiny bacteria» What is right? | No, a virus is much smaller and cannot copy itself without a cell | Yes, it is just a baby bacterium that has not grown up yet, but will | Yes, they are exactly the same thing | No, a virus is a kind of tiny insect
test: «ugh flu, 39 degrees, my body's broken» What is the fever really doing? | Helping fight the virus by making it harder to multiply | Showing the body has given up | Cooking the virus slowly in the blood until it is all dead | Nothing, fevers have no reason
test: «the doc gave me a jab, does that mean I've got the disease now» What is right? | No, the vaccine only shows the body a harmless piece or weak form of the germ | Yes, a vaccine always gives you the full illness for a while, so you get it over | Yes, but only for one week | No, a jab is just water
test: Count them: a cartoon virus is drawn with 12 spikes. 5 of them stick to a cell. How many spikes are still free? | 7 | 17 | 5 | 12
test: In a cartoon, a virus is a green monster with arms that punches cells. What is true? | A real virus has no arms or brain; it sticks to a cell and slips its instructions inside | Real viruses really do have tiny fists | Viruses are always green | Viruses chase cells across the body like hungry animals and bite them with their teeth
test: What would happen if almost everyone in a town was vaccinated against a disease? | The germ would find it very hard to spread | Everyone would catch the disease at once | The germ would grow stronger | Nothing would change at all
test: «bats carry viruses so we should get rid of all of them» What is best? | No, bats are vital for pollination and eating insects, and should be left alone | Yes, bats are only pests that spread germs and do nothing useful at all for anyone | Yes, but only the big ones | No, because bats will move into houses
test: Why is there no single vaccine that stops all colds? | There are over 200 different cold viruses | Colds are not caused by germs | Vaccines only work on animals | Colds are too small to vaccinate against
test: Granny had chickenpox as a girl and now has shingles. How can that be? | The chickenpox virus hid in her nerves for decades and woke up | She caught it again from a baby in her family | Shingles comes from eating bad food that has gone off in the fridge | Shingles is a kind of sunburn
test: Can a virus ever help as a medicine? | Yes, phages that attack bacteria are sometimes used when antibiotics fail | No, every single virus is always harmful to every living thing on Earth | Yes, the cold virus cures headaches | No, viruses cannot touch bacteria
test: In the 1630s a man paid as much as a house for one tulip bulb. What made his tulip special? | Stripy petals caused by a virus | It was made of real gold all the way through | It could flower all year round, even in snow | It was the only tulip in the world
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the status of viruses as living organisms remains a matter of scientific dispute. | They are incapable of growth or reproduction save within a living cell | They are composed entirely of mineral matter, as crystals of salt are | They are larger than any bacterium | They persist for no longer than one second
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the mode of action of a vaccine. | It presents to the immune system a harmless portion or weakened form of a pathogen, whereby recognition is acquired | It destroys every micro-organism within the body upon injection, both harmful and helpful alike, so that the body is clean | It induces the full disease in order that it may pass | It forms a protective coating upon the surface of each cell
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the history of smallpox? | Following the first vaccine of 1796, the disease was declared eradicated throughout the world in 1980 | It remains the most common illness of the present day | It was eradicated by antibiotics in 1928, shortly after their discovery by Fleming in London | It is identical with the common cold
exam: Ascertain the origin of the variegated petals of the so-called «broken» tulips of the seventeenth century. | Infection of the plants by a virus | The application of paint by florists | Cultivation in exceptionally cold soil | The crossing of tulips with garden roses
